nodecode-hindsight is a rewrite of vectorize-io's vectorize-io/hindsight:

  https://github.com/vectorize-io/hindsight
  commit f14cc1eb5624b0374cb4d82768eb2e0b9c21ae24
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Vectorize AI, Inc. (LICENSE)

The original is hindsight-integrations/hermes, a Hermes Agent memory-provider
plugin. What was rewritten: the plugin's three model-facing tools
(hindsight_retain, hindsight_recall, hindsight_reflect) and the automatic
halves around them (auto-recall of a turn's input, auto-retain of the
operator's words), in Common Lisp against the same Hindsight HTTP API --
POST /v1/default/banks/{bank}/memories/recall, /memories and /reflect -- so
the cell talks to the same service and the same bank. The plugin's
configuration surface (endpoint, API key, bank, budget, recall token cap,
timeouts, tags) is the cell's :section, and the reader is /hindsight plus
(help :hindsight).

What was left out, and why: the Python SDK (hindsight-client and
hindsight-embed) and the local-embedded daemon manager -- the cell speaks
HTTP itself and reaches a server either in Hindsight Cloud or one the operator
runs, without downloading a Python package; Hermes' config.json /
profile .env layering, its setup wizard, its indicator lines and its
per-process retain queue with backlog resend; the multi-bank and
workspace-scoped bank features (mirror_to_own_bank, additional_banks,
recall_additional_banks, trusted_project_dirs with a repository's
.hindsight/config.toml); and the fine retain/recall tuning knobs (retain
strategies and prefixes, observation scopes, minimum scores, per-kind fact
filters). Automatic retain here stores the operator's own turn once, since
Nodecode's hook vocabulary has no turn-end point; the assistant's half of an
exchange is what hindsight_retain is for.
